    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chile's independence grew out of the crisis of the Spanish monarchy after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1808. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> a governing junta formed on 18 September 1810, a date still celebrated as the national holiday, opening the period known as the Patria Vieja, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-miguel-carrera">José Miguel Carrera</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins">Bernardo O'Higgins</a> quarrelled while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-fernando-de-abascal">José Fernando de Abascal</a> sent royalist forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. The royalist victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rancagua">Battle of Rancagua</a> in October 1814 restored Spanish rule, and many patriots fled across the Andes to Mendoza, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuyo">Cuyo</a>, was building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-andes">Army of the Andes</a>. In January 1817 that army crossed the mountain passes and defeated the royalists at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chacabuco">Battle of Chacabuco</a> on 12 February 1817. O'Higgins became Supreme Director, and the act of independence, dated 1 January 1818, was proclaimed on 12 February 1818, the anniversary of Chacabuco. After a surprise defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-cancha-rayada">Cancha Rayada</a> in March, San Martín and O'Higgins destroyed the main royalist army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maipu">Battle of Maipú</a> on 5 April 1818, securing central Chile. A Chilean navy, commanded from 1818 by the British admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cochrane-10th-earl-of-dundonald">Thomas Cochrane</a>, then carried San Martín's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberating-expedition-of-peru">Liberating Expedition</a> to Peru in 1820, and the last royalist stronghold, the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiloe">Chiloé</a>, fell in 1826.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valparaiso.jpg" alt="Valparaíso" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Historic_Quarter_of_the_Seaport_City_of_Valpara%C3%ADso_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Julia Sumangil, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nineteenth-century Chile was a society of great landed estates, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a></em> of the Central Valley, where a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> elite ruled over tenant labourers known as <em>inquilinos</em>, while mining in the arid north provided most export earnings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> strike at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanarcillo">Chañarcillo</a> in 1832 and expanding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mining made Chile one of the world's leading copper producers by the mid-century, and its wheat fed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> during their gold rushes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a> became the main commercial port of the South American Pacific. The conquest of the nitrate fields in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> gave the state rich export taxes for half a century, until synthetic nitrates and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>, which hit Chile among the hardest of any country, ended the boom. Copper, mined largely by American companies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-teniente">El Teniente</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuquicamata">Chuquicamata</a>, then became the main export; it was nationalised in 1971 and later placed under the state company <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codelco">Codelco</a>. The Catholic Church was the established church until 1925, and secular laws on cemeteries and civil marriage in the 1880s weakened its hold. From the 1970s the military regime's economists, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chicago-boys">Chicago Boys</a>, opened the economy, privatised state firms and pensions and promoted exports of fruit, wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and fish. Copper and lithium remain central, and Chile is one of the richest countries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, though marked by high inequality.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yungay"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-yungay.jpg" alt="Battle of Yungay" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yungay">Battle of Yungay</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plano_de_la_Batalla_de_Yungay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Chilean armed forces were among the most effective in nineteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. Under the conservative order of the 1830s Chile fought the War of the Confederation against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>–Bolivian Confederation, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-portales">Diego Portales</a> regarded as a threat; after Portales was murdered by mutinous soldiers in 1837, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-bulnes">Manuel Bulnes</a> won the decisive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yungay">Battle of Yungay</a> in January 1839, ending the confederation. In 1865–1866 Chile and Peru fought a Spanish squadron, which bombarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a> in 1866. The greatest conflict was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> of 1879–1883 against Peru and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, fought over nitrate deposits. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arturo-prat">Captain Arturo Prat</a> died at the naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-iquique">Battle of Iquique</a> and the Peruvian ironclad <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a></em> was captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-angamos">Angamos</a> in 1879, Chile won command of the sea, invaded Peru and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in 1881. At home the army completed the occupation of Araucanía, subduing the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> by 1883. From the late 1880s the German officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emil-korner">Emil Körner</a> reorganised the army on Prussian lines, and the navy became one of the strongest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, until a naval arms race with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> was halted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacts-of-may">Pacts of May</a> in 1902. In the twentieth century the armed forces intervened in politics in 1924–1925 and again in the coup of 1973, and a near war with Argentina over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beagle-channel">Beagle Channel</a> in 1978 was averted by papal mediation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chilean-civil-code"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chilean-civil-code.jpg" alt="Chilean Civil Code" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chilean-civil-code">Chilean Civil Code</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codigo_Civil_Chile_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardo-ohiggins">O'Higgins</a>, who abdicated in 1823, Chile experimented with federal and liberal constitutions until conservative forces won the civil war of 1829–1830 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lircay">Battle of Lircay</a>. The minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-portales">Diego Portales</a>, though never president, then shaped a strong, centralised presidential state, enshrined in the Constitution of 1833, which lasted until 1925. Four presidents, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joaquin-prieto">Joaquín Prieto</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-joaquin-perez">José Joaquín Pérez</a>, each served two consecutive five-year terms between 1831 and 1871, giving Chile a stability rare in nineteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-bello">Andrés Bello</a>, first rector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chile">University of Chile</a>, founded in 1842, drafted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilean-civil-code">Civil Code of 1855</a>. After the civil war of 1891, in which Congress defeated President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-manuel-balmaceda">José Manuel Balmaceda</a>, the period known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-republic-chile">Parliamentary Republic</a> followed, until the Constitution of 1925 restored presidential power. Women won the vote in national elections in 1949. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a> government elected in 1938 created the development agency <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corfo">CORFO</a> in 1939, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eduardo-frei-montalva">Christian Democrat Eduardo Frei Montalva</a> expanded land reform in the 1960s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende">Salvador Allende</a>, elected in 1970 at the head of the Popular Unity coalition, was overthrown on 11 September 1973 by the armed forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-pinochet">Augusto Pinochet</a>, whose regime killed or caused the disappearance of more than 3,000 people and imprisoned and tortured tens of thousands. Its Constitution of 1980, much amended since, remains in force.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/antofagasta"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antofagasta.jpg" alt="Antofagasta" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/antofagasta">Antofagasta</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montaje_de_Antofagasta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Montaje realizado por Açipni-Lovrij., CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chile's long constitutional stability in the nineteenth century was often contrasted with the turmoil of its neighbours, and its institutions influenced the region: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilean-civil-code">Andrés Bello's Civil Code</a> was adopted or adapted in Ecuador, Colombia and elsewhere, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chile">University of Chile</a> became a leading centre of learning. Victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> reshaped the map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> without a coastline, a loss Bolivia has never accepted, and giving Chile the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarapaca">Tarapacá</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antofagasta">Antofagasta</a>, while the dispute over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a> was settled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> only by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lima-1929">Treaty of Lima</a> of 1929. The annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Easter Island</a>, Rapa Nui, in 1888 extended Chile into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. Chilean literature gained world recognition: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriela-mistral">Gabriela Mistral</a> became the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> American winner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1945, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-neruda">Pablo Neruda</a> followed in 1971. The election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende">Salvador Allende</a>, often described as the first Marxist to win a presidency through free elections, and his overthrow in 1973 became a defining episode of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. The crimes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-pinochet">Pinochet</a> regime led to the former dictator's arrest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1998, a landmark in international human rights law, while the regime's free-market reforms, including a private pension system, were studied, copied and fiercely debated around the world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific.jpg" alt="War of the Pacific" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:War_of_the_Pacific_LOC_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">United States Federal Government, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic of Chile has existed continuously since 1818, though its territory and institutions changed greatly. It reached roughly its present shape after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> and the occupation of Araucanía, while the boundary treaty of 1881 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tierra-del-fuego">Tierra del Fuego</a> between the two countries. The military regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-pinochet">Augusto Pinochet</a> lost a plebiscite on 5 October 1988, when about 56 per cent of voters rejected another eight years of his rule, and the Christian Democrat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patricio-aylwin">Patricio Aylwin</a> took office in 1990 at the head of the centre-left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concertacion">Concertación</a>, which governed for twenty years. Pinochet remained army commander until 1998, and reforms in 2005 removed the most authoritarian features of the 1980 constitution. Chile grew rapidly, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oecd">OECD</a> in 2010 and cut poverty sharply, but inequality and the cost of services fuelled mass protests in October 2019, the <em>estallido social</em>. In a referendum in 2020 voters approved drafting a new constitution, yet the texts produced by an elected convention and by a later council were rejected in referendums in 2022 and 2023. Today Chile is a presidential republic of about 20 million people, the world's largest producer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and a major producer of lithium, with democratic institutions that remain among the most stable in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Republic of Chile combined unusual constitutional stability with military success, winning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> and growing rich on nitrates and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>; its democracy, broken by the 1973 coup and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-pinochet">Pinochet</a>’s dictatorship, was restored in 1990 and remains among the most durable in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>.</em></p>

